  "instruction": "Read the passage and then answer the question.",
  "passage": "The village of Hollin Mere sits at the head of a long glacial lake. For most of its history the only road in ran along the eastern shore, and it flooded every spring when the snowmelt raised the lake by a metre or more. In 1962 the county built a causeway across the narrow northern end, which shortened the journey to the market town from forty minutes to twelve and let the dairy co-operative send milk out twice a day instead of once. The causeway also changed the lake. Water on the sheltered side warmed earlier, reeds spread along the new still margins, and the pike that had spawned in the open shallows moved north. Anglers complained, the co-operative prospered, and the village school, which had been about to close, stayed open because young families could now commute. In the 1990s a study found that the causeway's culverts were too small to let the lake mix, and the sheltered basin had begun to lose oxygen in late summer. The county replaced two culverts with a short bridge in 2004. Within five years the reeds had thinned, the pike had partly returned, and the spring floods on the old shore road, which nobody used any more, became a tourist curiosity. The lighthouse at Point Harrow was built in 1854 after three ships were wrecked on the reef in a single winter. Its first keeper, Eliza Marsh, kept the lamp burning for forty-one years and recorded every passing vessel in a ledger that still survives. The lamp was converted from whale oil to kerosene in 1887 and to electricity in 1931. The keepers' families grew vegetables on the lee side of the rock, where a stone wall kept the salt spray off, and once a month a supply boat brought flour, lamp parts and letters. In 1912 a storm carried away the landing stage, and for six weeks the keeper's son rowed out to meet the boat in open water. The lighthouse was automated in 1972, and the keeper's cottage is now a small museum open on summer weekends, where the ledger lies open under glass at the page for the night the last ship was lost.",
